{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The hip joint is a synovial, multiaxial ball-and-socket joint between the head of the femur and the acetabulum, which is deepened by the fibrocartilaginous labrum. Stability comes from the capsule, the iliofemoral, pubofemoral and ischiofemoral ligaments, the labrum and the surrounding muscles. In a child the femoral head depends mainly on retinacular branches of the medial circumflex femoral artery.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN17.2, AN17.3" phase="Phase 1" /%}

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/hip-joint/hip-joint-coronal-section-overview.webp" alt="Labelled coronal section of the hip joint showing the femoral head in the acetabulum, the labrum, the capsule and the ligament of the head of the femur." caption="Coronal section of the hip joint showing the femoral head seated in the acetabulum within its capsule." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grierson_5_Cadera.JPG" width="960" height="1084" /%}

## What kind of joint is the hip?

The hip is a **synovial, multiaxial, ball-and-socket joint**. "Multiaxial" means it moves around more than one axis. The ball is the head of the femur and the socket is the acetabulum of the hip bone. The hip has to be both stable and flexible, because it carries the body in standing and walking.

It allows a wide range of movement, yet it dislocates much less often than the shoulder.

## Bones and articular surfaces

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/hip-joint/femur-neck-shaft-angle-coxa-vara-valga.webp" alt="Diagram of three femurs showing a normal neck-shaft angle, coxa vara with a smaller angle and coxa valga with a larger angle." caption="The neck-shaft angle is smaller in coxa vara and larger in coxa valga than normal." credit="Behrang Amini, MD/PhD, Zyryab, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 2.5" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FemurAngles.jpg" width="835" height="571" /%}

- **Acetabulum:** the cup-shaped socket, formed at the junction of the ilium, ischium and pubis. On its own it covers slightly less than a hemisphere. Its articular surface is a horseshoe-shaped area covered with hyaline cartilage. The floor is non-articular and is called the **acetabular fossa**. The lower margin is deficient and forms the **acetabular notch**.
- **Femoral head:** almost spherical, but not quite, so none of the movement axes is truly fixed. It is covered with hyaline cartilage and fits closely in an area of the socket that is more than half a sphere. A small pit on the head, the **fovea capitis**, gives attachment to the ligament of the head of the femur.
- **Neck of femur:** the head narrows gradually into the neck, like a light bulb. This tapering limits contact stress between the neck and the acetabular margin during extreme movements.

The angle between the shaft and the neck of the femur is the **neck-shaft angle**. Standard references give slightly different adult values (an average of 135 degrees in one, about 127 degrees in another), so check which figure your department uses. In a child it is about 150 degrees. A reduced angle is **coxa vara** and an increased angle is **coxa valga**.

## Acetabular labrum

The **acetabular labrum** is a fibrocartilaginous rim attached to the margin of the acetabulum. It narrows the mouth of the socket and deepens it, which helps hold the head in place. Nerve endings in the labrum suggest it gives proprioception (position sense) and can be a source of pain when injured.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/hip-joint/acetabulum-labrum-lateral-view.webp" alt="Labelled view into the acetabulum showing the labrum, transverse acetabular ligament, acetabular notch, fossa and the lunate articular surface." caption="The acetabulum seen from the side, with the labrum and transverse ligament completing the rim over the notch." credit="BodyParts3D is made by DBCLS, CC BY-SA 2.1 jp, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 2.1 jp" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lunate_Surface_Of_Acetabulum_04_lateral_view_(Right_hip_bone).png" width="960" height="960" /%}

The **transverse acetabular ligament** is the part of the labrum that bridges the acetabular notch. It turns the notch into a foramen through which vessels and nerves enter the joint.

## Capsule and synovial membrane

The fibrous capsule is strong and dense. It is attached to the acetabular margin, the labrum and the transverse ligament. On the femur it is attached to the intertrochanteric line in front and to the neck behind, stopping short of the intertrochanteric crest.

- **Two sets of fibres:** the outer fibres are longitudinal. The inner fibres are circular and form a collar round the femoral neck, called the **zona orbicularis**. These circular fibres are not attached directly to bone.
- **Thick in front and above:** the capsule is thick and firmly attached anterosuperiorly, where tension is greatest in standing. It is thin and loose posteroinferiorly.
- **Retinacula:** many longitudinal fibres are reflected up along the neck as bands called **retinacula**. The blood vessels to the head and neck run along them.
- **Epiphysis inside the capsule:** the capsule is attached well below the growth plate of the femoral head, so the upper femoral epiphysis is entirely intracapsular.

The **synovial membrane** lines the capsule and the intracapsular part of the neck. It also covers both surfaces of the labrum, the transverse ligament and the fat in the acetabular fossa, and it ensheaths the ligament of the head of the femur.

The joint cavity communicates with the bursa deep to the tendon of psoas major through a circular opening between the pubofemoral ligament and the vertical band of the iliofemoral ligament. Other bursae lie at the greater trochanter, around the gluteal tendons, and between gluteus maximus and vastus lateralis.

## Ligaments

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/hip-joint/hip-ligaments-anterior-posterior.webp" alt="Labelled anterior and posterior views of the hip showing the iliofemoral, pubofemoral and ischiofemoral ligaments." caption="The three capsular ligaments of the hip seen from the front and from behind." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sobo_1909_208.png" width="960" height="705" /%}

{% table %}
- **Ligament**
- **Attachments and key points**
- **What it does**
---
- **Iliofemoral (inverted Y, ligament of Bigelow)**
- Lies in front, blended with the capsule. The apex is attached to the anterior inferior iliac spine and the base to the intertrochanteric line. The upper oblique and lower vertical bands are thick and strong, and the middle fibres are thin. One of the strongest ligaments in the body.
- Stops the trunk falling backwards in standing and limits extension. Also limits lateral rotation.
---
- **Pubofemoral**
- Triangular. The base is attached to the superior pubic ramus (with the obturator crest and membrane) and the apex to the lower part of the intertrochanteric line.
- Supports the joint inferomedially. Limits extension, abduction and lateral rotation.
---
- **Ischiofemoral**
- Spiral. Attached to the body of the ischium near the acetabular margin. The fibres pass up and laterally to the greater trochanter, and some blend with the zona orbicularis.
- Limits extension and medial rotation
---
- **Transverse acetabular**
- Part of the labrum bridging the acetabular notch
- Converts the notch into a foramen for vessels and nerves
---
- **Ligament of the head of the femur**
- Flat and triangular. The apex is attached to the fovea capitis and the base to the transverse ligament and the margins of the acetabular notch. It lies inside the joint, covered by synovium, and varies in strength.
- Carries a branch of the obturator artery to the head. Limits adduction. Its stabilising role in the adult is uncertain and is secondary to the capsular ligaments and muscles.
{% /table %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**One fact to hold on to:** all three capsular ligaments (iliofemoral, pubofemoral, ischiofemoral) limit **extension**. That is why the ligaments are tightest when the hip is extended.
{% /callout %}

The ligament of the head of the femur is taut when the hip is semi-flexed, laterally rotated and adducted. This is the position in which the capsular ligaments give the least stability.

## What keeps the hip stable?

- **Static stabilisers:** the capsule, the ligaments and the labrum.
- **Dynamic stabilisers:** the muscles that act across the joint.
- **Bony fit:** the head sits deep in a socket that is more than half a sphere.
- **Vacuum effect:** suction inside the joint resists separation. Strong traction pulls the surfaces apart only slightly. Before hip arthroscopy a needle is passed into the joint to remove this suction.

Because of all this, a great force is needed to dislocate the hip.

## Relations

- **In front:** the tendon of psoas major, with iliacus lateral to it, separated from the capsule partly by a bursa. Pectineus separates the capsule from the femoral vein. The femoral artery lies in front of the psoas tendon, and the femoral nerve lies in a groove between the psoas tendon and iliacus. The straight head of rectus femoris crosses the joint.
- **Above:** the reflected head of rectus femoris and gluteus minimus.
- **Below and behind:** obturator externus spirals to the back of the capsule. Obturator internus and the gemelli lie against the capsule and separate it from the sciatic nerve.

## Movements and muscles

The axes all pass through the centre of the femoral head:

- **Flexion and extension** occur around a transverse axis.
- **Abduction and adduction** occur around an anteroposterior axis.
- **Medial and lateral rotation** occur around a vertical axis.
- **Circumduction** is a combination of all these movements.

The ranges given are extension 15 degrees, abduction 50 degrees, medial rotation 25 degrees and lateral rotation 60 degrees. Another description gives flexion of about 120 degrees and extension of 10 to 20 degrees beyond the plane of the trunk.

{% table %}
- **Movement**
- **Chief muscles**
- **Accessory muscles and notes**
---
- **Flexion**
- Psoas major and iliacus
- Pectineus, rectus femoris, sartorius; adductors (mainly adductor longus) in the early part. Iliacus is supplied by the femoral nerve.
---
- **Extension**
- Gluteus maximus and hamstrings
- Adductor magnus helps. Gluteus maximus (inferior gluteal nerve) is the chief extensor and is vital in rising from sitting.
---
- **Abduction**
- Gluteus medius and minimus
- Tensor fasciae latae and sartorius. Supplied by the superior gluteal nerve.
---
- **Adduction**
- Adductor longus, brevis and magnus
- Pectineus and gracilis
---
- **Medial rotation**
- Tensor fasciae latae and the anterior fibres of gluteus medius and minimus
- Weaker than the lateral rotators
---
- **Lateral rotation**
- The two obturators, the two gemelli and quadratus femoris
- Piriformis, gluteus maximus and sartorius. More powerful than the medial rotators.
{% /table %}

**Limits to movement.** Flexion with the knee bent is stopped when the thigh meets the anterior abdominal wall. With the knee straight, tension in the hamstrings stops flexion earlier. With the knee fully flexed, tension in rectus femoris, stretched at both hip and knee, can stop the hip from reaching full extension. Adduction is limited by contact with the other limb and by the ligament of the head of the femur.

**Pelvis on femur.** When you stand, the femur is fixed and the pelvis moves on it. An anterior pelvic tilt is equivalent to hip flexion and a posterior tilt is equivalent to hip extension.

**Two key positions:**

- **Close-packed position:** extension with slight abduction and medial rotation. The ligaments and capsule are tightest here.
- **Most congruent position:** slight flexion, abduction and lateral rotation. The joint surfaces fit best here, and joint capacity is greatest. A patient with a hip effusion (excess fluid in the joint) is most comfortable in this position, and so is a patient with hip arthritis.

## Biomechanics and the abductor mechanism

The load across the hip is large, mainly because of muscle contraction across the joint:

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/hip-joint/single-leg-stance-abductor-mechanism.webp" alt="Diagram of single-leg stance showing the abductor muscles balancing body weight around the hip, and a pelvic dip when they fail." caption="The abductors of the standing leg hold the pelvis level, and a dip on the other side is a positive Trendelenburg sign." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Figure_89_applied_anatomy_and_kinesiology_1917.jpg" width="960" height="1354" /%}

- Lifting the leg from the bed: about one and a half times body weight
- Standing on one leg: about three times body weight
- Running and jumping: about ten times body weight

When you stand on one leg, gluteus medius and minimus on the standing side stop the opposite side of the pelvis from tilting down. They act through the hip joint, the neck of the femur and the abductor muscles together, called the **abductor lever arm**. If the mechanism fails, the pelvis dips and the patient has a positive **Trendelenburg sign** (a dipping gait). The mechanism is disturbed in hip dislocation and fracture of the neck of the femur.

## Blood supply (AN17.2)

**Joint as a whole:** articular arteries come from the obturator, medial circumflex femoral, and superior and inferior gluteal arteries. They form the cruciate and trochanteric anastomoses. The veins correspond.

**Femoral head and neck:**

- The medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries form an arterial circle round the capsular attachment on the neck.
- **Retinacular arteries** arise from this circle and run up along the retinacula under the synovium. They supply the intracapsular neck and the greater part of the head. The medial circumflex femoral artery is the main source.
- A small part of the head near the fovea is supplied by acetabular branches of the obturator and medial circumflex femoral arteries. The artery of the ligament of the head is a branch of the obturator artery.

**Child versus adult:** in a child the growth plate (**physis**) is avascular, so the head depends on the retinacular vessels, which pass round the edge of the physis. The artery of the ligament is only a minor contributor. While the epiphyseal cartilage remains, the two sources do not communicate. After it disappears in the adult, an anastomosis forms between them.

This is why damage to the retinacular vessels, from trauma or infection, can cause **avascular necrosis** (bone death from loss of blood supply) of the femoral head.

**Lymphatics:** the front of the joint drains to the deep inguinal nodes. The medial and posterior parts drain to the internal iliac nodes along the obturator and gluteal arteries.

## Nerve supply

- **Anterior capsule:** femoral, obturator and accessory obturator (when present) nerves.
- **Posterior capsule:** sciatic and superior gluteal nerves, and the nerve to quadratus femoris.

The **obturator nerve** arises from the ventral divisions of the ventral rami of L2, L3 and L4. The **femoral nerve** arises from the dorsal divisions of the same segments. Both supply the hip and the knee.

**Hilton's law** states that a joint is supplied by the same nerves as the muscles that move it. Because the hip and knee share nerves, pain from one joint can be felt in the other. This is **referred pain**, pain felt at a site away from its source. Disease of the hip can also be felt in the front and medial side of the thigh.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Child with knee pain?** Always examine the hip. Hip disease can present as knee pain because the femoral and obturator nerves supply both joints.
{% /callout %}

## Development in brief

The hip joint begins to develop at about the 7th week of gestation, when a cleft appears in the mesenchyme of the limb bud. By the 11th week the femoral head and acetabulum are fully formed in cartilage. At birth the acetabulum is entirely cartilage, with a thin rim of fibrocartilage, the labrum. It is relatively large and shallow. The whole upper femur is cartilaginous in the newborn.

## Applied anatomy: dislocation (AN17.3)

Hip dislocations are classified by the direction of displacement of the femoral head:

- **Posterior:** the commonest type, about 80% of cases
- **Anterior**
- **Central:** a fracture-dislocation through the floor of the acetabulum

**Posterior dislocation (dashboard injury).** The force is directed along the femoral shaft with the hip flexed, often in a car passenger whose knee strikes the dashboard. The head is forced backwards out of the socket. A piece of the posterior lip of the acetabulum is often sheared off, which makes it a fracture-dislocation. In an isolated posterior dislocation the leg is shortened, adducted, internally rotated and slightly flexed. The head may be felt in the gluteal region.

**Complications of posterior dislocation:**

- **Sciatic nerve injury:** usually a neurapraxia (a temporary nerve injury) that recovers on its own.
- **Avascular necrosis** of the femoral head in some 15 to 20 per cent of cases. X-ray changes usually appear 1 to 2 years after injury.
- **Osteoarthritis:** a late complication, years after the injury, often needing hip replacement.
- **Myositis ossificans** (new bone in muscle): weeks to months after injury, with persistent pain and stiffness.

**Congenital dislocation** is more common in the hip than in any other joint. The head slips up onto the gluteal surface of the ilium because the upper margin of the acetabulum is developmentally deficient. It causes a lurching gait and a positive Trendelenburg test.

## Applied anatomy: fracture neck of femur (AN17.2)

There are two types:

- **Intracapsular:** this is what is usually meant by "fracture of the neck of the femur". It can be subcapital (near the head), cervical (middle) or basal (near the trochanters).
- **Extracapsular:** usually called an **intertrochanteric fracture**.

They are separated because they behave differently. An intracapsular fracture almost never unites, whereas an intertrochanteric fracture unites readily. In both, the distal fragment is typically externally rotated and moves upwards. The displacement is more marked in the intertrochanteric fracture. In an intracapsular fracture the capsule is attached to the distal fragment and prevents extreme rotation and displacement. An extracapsular fracture lies outside the capsular attachment, so nothing stops wide displacement.

Damage to the retinacular arteries causes avascular necrosis of the head.

## Avascular necrosis of the femoral head

Avascular necrosis (AVN) follows fracture of the neck of the femur or posterior dislocation of the hip. The dead bone deforms and collapses, and secondary osteoarthritis follows a few years later, with painful limitation of movement.

- **Early picture:** pain and stiffness appear late. Radiographs show a dense (sclerotic) area, later collapse. A bone scan can show a "cold area" before X-ray changes.
- **Adults without injury:** AVN usually affects men aged 35 to 45 and is bilateral in over 50% of patients. Early on the patient may have no symptoms. Later there is a groin ache and a limp. The radiographic **crescent sign** shows subchondral bone resorption. MRI is the most sensitive and specific investigation.

## Hip disease by age and common disorders

{% table %}
- **Age**
- **Condition**
---
- **Below 5 years**
- Congenital dislocation and tuberculosis
---
- **5 to 10 years**
- Perthes' disease: destruction and flattening of the femoral head with an increased joint space on X-ray. Onset before 7 years has a reasonably good prognosis.
---
- **10 to 20 years**
- Coxa vara. Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE) also occurs in adolescents.
{% /table %}

**Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE).** The physis joins the femoral head (epiphysis) to the neck (metaphysis). In SCFE this growth plate fails and the head displaces relative to the neck.

- **Who:** adolescents, most often 10 to 15 or 16 years. It is commoner in boys, and the peak is at the start of puberty, so it comes earlier in girls. Obesity and repeated minor trauma precipitate a slip. Hypothyroidism, renal failure and previous radiotherapy raise the risk.
- **Presentation:** the chronic form is the commonest. The adolescent has a few months of vague groin, thigh or knee pain and a limp. The leg lies short and externally rotated, and hip flexion and external rotation are limited.
- **Classification:** acute, chronic or acute-on-chronic by timing of symptoms, and stable or unstable by whether the child can bear weight.
- **Management:** once diagnosed, the child is admitted and put on bed rest. The aim of treatment is to keep the femoral head spherical.

**Labral tears and impingement.** Tears of the labrum are among the commonest internal derangements of the hip and cause pain and mechanical symptoms. MRI after injecting contrast into the joint is the gold standard. In **femoroacetabular impingement**, a **cam deformity** (loss of the normal taper at the head-neck junction) causes the neck to jam against the anterosuperior labrum, typically in flexion, medial rotation and adduction.

**Osteoarthritis.** Causes are grouped as mechanical or non-mechanical:

- **Mechanical causes:** abnormal loads follow loss of the matched spherical fit. Examples are developmental dysplasia (under-coverage of the head), femoroacetabular impingement (over-coverage or abnormal femoral shape), Perthes disease, SCFE and past fractures.
- **Non-mechanical causes:** avascular necrosis, ankylosing spondylitis and inflammatory arthritis.

## Clinical points on examination

- **Where pain is felt:** true hip pain is deep in the crease of the groin. Pain over the lateral hip usually suggests trochanteric bursitis. Buttock pain is only occasionally from the hip. It is more often referred from the lumbosacral spine or caused by vascular claudication.
- **Palpating the head:** the part of the head not inside the socket can be felt just below the inguinal ligament, lateral to the femoral artery pulse. Tenderness here usually means hip arthritis.
- **Leg length:** it is measured from the anterior superior iliac spine to the medial malleolus. Injuries around the hip can shorten the limb.
- **Aspiration:** a needle is passed from a point 5 cm below the anterior superior iliac spine, upwards, backwards and medially.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The iliofemoral ligament is the inverted Y-shaped ligament in front of the joint. It is one of the strongest ligaments in the body and stops the trunk falling backwards in standing." %}
Which ligament of the hip is shaped like an inverted Y and prevents the trunk from falling backwards in standing?
- A. Pubofemoral ligament
- B. Iliofemoral ligament
- C. Ischiofemoral ligament
- D. Ligament of the head of the femur
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="In a child the growth plate is avascular, so the head depends mainly on retinacular vessels from the medial circumflex femoral artery, which run round the edge of the physis. Damage to them can cause avascular necrosis. The artery of the ligament of the head is only a minor contributor." %}
Why is the femoral head of a child at risk of avascular necrosis after injury to the femoral neck?
- A. The artery of the ligament of the head supplies most of the head
- B. Vessels cross the growth plate freely into the head
- C. The growth plate is avascular, so the head depends mainly on retinacular vessels from the medial circumflex femoral artery
- D. The head has no blood supply until adulthood
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="In an isolated posterior dislocation, the commonest type, the leg is shortened, adducted, internally rotated and slightly flexed. A short, externally rotated leg suggests a fracture of the neck of the femur or a slipped epiphysis instead." %}
A car passenger's knee strikes the dashboard. Which limb position is expected in an isolated posterior dislocation of the hip?
- A. Shortened, adducted, internally rotated and slightly flexed
- B. Lengthened, abducted and externally rotated
- C. Shortened and externally rotated
- D. Lengthened, adducted and internally rotated
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Hilton's law says a joint is supplied by the nerves that supply the muscles moving it. The femoral and obturator nerves supply both the hip and the knee, so hip disease can be felt as knee pain." %}
A child with a slipped capital femoral epiphysis complains mainly of knee pain. The best explanation is:
- A. The hip and knee share one joint capsule
- B. The hip and knee drain to the same lymph nodes
- C. The sciatic nerve is the only nerve supplying both joints
- D. The femoral and obturator nerves supply both joints, as explained by Hilton's law
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="In an intracapsular fracture the capsule is attached to the distal fragment and prevents extreme rotation and displacement. An intertrochanteric fracture lies outside the capsular attachment, so nothing limits displacement." %}
Displacement is usually less marked in an intracapsular fracture of the neck of the femur than in an intertrochanteric fracture because:
- A. The ligament of the head holds the fragments together
- B. The capsule is attached to the distal fragment and limits rotation and displacement
- C. Gluteus maximus holds the distal fragment in place
- D. The fracture lies below the capsular attachment
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Gluteus medius and minimus on the standing side keep the pelvis level when the opposite foot is off the ground. They are supplied by the superior gluteal nerve. Failure of this mechanism gives a positive Trendelenburg sign." %}
Which muscles, with which nerve supply, keep the pelvis level when you stand on one leg?
- A. Gluteus maximus, inferior gluteal nerve
- B. Iliopsoas, femoral nerve
- C. Gluteus medius and minimus, superior gluteal nerve
- D. Adductor magnus, obturator nerve
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What type of joint is the hip?" %}
A synovial, multiaxial ball-and-socket joint between the head of the femur and the acetabulum. The acetabulum is deepened by the labrum.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What keeps the hip stable?" %}
Static stabilisers: the capsule, the ligaments (iliofemoral, pubofemoral, ischiofemoral) and the labrum. Dynamic stabilisers: the muscles across the joint. The deep socket and the vacuum effect also help.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the iliofemoral ligament." %}
An inverted Y-shaped ligament in front of the joint, blended with the capsule. The apex is on the anterior inferior iliac spine and the base on the intertrochanteric line. It is one of the strongest ligaments in the body, stops the trunk falling backwards in standing and limits extension.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the blood supply of the head of the femur." %}
The medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries form a circle round the neck. Retinacular arteries from it, mainly from the medial circumflex, supply the neck and most of the head. A small part near the fovea is supplied through the ligament of the head by a branch of the obturator artery. In a child the growth plate is avascular, so damage to the retinacular vessels can cause avascular necrosis.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which nerves supply the hip, and why can hip disease cause knee pain?" %}
The femoral, obturator and accessory obturator nerves supply the anterior capsule. The sciatic and superior gluteal nerves and the nerve to quadratus femoris supply the posterior capsule. The femoral and obturator nerves also supply the knee, so by Hilton's law pain is referred between the two joints.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe posterior dislocation of the hip and its complications." %}
It is the commonest type of dislocation, often a dashboard injury with the force along the femoral shaft and the hip flexed. The leg is shortened, adducted, internally rotated and slightly flexed. Complications are sciatic nerve injury, avascular necrosis of the head, osteoarthritis and myositis ossificans.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the abductor mechanism, and what is Trendelenburg's sign?" %}
Gluteus medius and minimus act through the hip joint and femoral neck as a lever. They stop the opposite side of the pelvis tilting down when you stand on one leg. If it fails, as in dislocation or fracture of the neck of the femur, the pelvis dips and the Trendelenburg sign is positive.
{% /viva %}

